.Dd October 3, 2026
.Dt FALLOCATE 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm fallocate
.Nd allocate a range of a file ahead of its writes
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In fcntl.h
.Ft int
.Fn fallocate "int fd" "int mode" "off_t offset" "off_t len"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn fallocate
gives the range of
.Fa len
bytes at
.Fa offset
of a regular file open for writing its storage ahead of the writes to it,
and grows the file to the range's end unless
.Fa mode
has
.Dv FALLOC_FL_KEEP_SIZE .
On a tmpfs the range's pages are made, zeroed, counted against the
mount's
.Ql size= ,
as on Linux, and
.Dv FALLOC_FL_PUNCH_HOLE
with
.Dv FALLOC_FL_KEEP_SIZE
makes the range read as zeros and frees the pages it covers whole; a
growth the grow seal forbids, or a hole in a file sealed against writing,
fails with
.Er EPERM .
On ext2, which keeps no block reserved ahead of its data, each hole in
the range is given a block by a write of zeros, where Linux's ext2 answers
.Er EOPNOTSUPP ;
with
.Dv FALLOC_FL_KEEP_SIZE
the range stops at the file's size, and a punched hole is
.Er EOPNOTSUPP .
Another file system and a block device fail with
.Er EOPNOTSUPP ,
Linux's answer for a file without the operation.
glibc's
.Fn posix_fallocate
then writes the range itself, as it does on Linux's ext2; it does not
after
.Er ENOSYS ,
the error the call gave before it was registered.
musl's
.Fn posix_fallocate
returns the error.
.Pp
The i386 system call number is 324, with the descriptor in EBX, the mode in
ECX, the offset in EDX and ESI and the length in EDI and EBP, each low word
first.
On x86-64 the number is 285, with the descriptor, the mode, the offset and
the length in RDI, RSI, RDX and R10.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
The call returns -1 with
.Va errno
set; the kernel returns the negative error number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/musl/callsmusl.c
requires the range's pages and blocks of a file in
.Pa /dev/shm ,
.Er EOPNOTSUPP
of a KRFFS file open for writing,
.Er EBADF
of one open only for reading,
.Er EINVAL
of a length of 0, and
.Er ESPIPE
of a pipe.
.Sh ERRORS
The checks are made in Linux's order:
.Bl -tag -width "[EOPNOTSUPP]"
.It Bq Er EBADF
.Fa fd
is not open, or was opened with
.Dv O_PATH .
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fa offset
is negative or
.Fa len
is not positive.
.It Bq Er EOPNOTSUPP
.Fa mode
is not one Linux takes: more than one of its modes, a hole punched without
.Dv FALLOC_FL_KEEP_SIZE ,
or a range collapsed, inserted or zeroed by writes with it.
.It Bq Er EBADF
.Fa fd
is not open for writing.
.It Bq Er ESPIPE
.Fa fd
is a pipe.
.It Bq Er EISDIR
.Fa fd
is a directory.
.It Bq Er ENODEV
.Fa fd
is not a regular file or a block device.
.It Bq Er EFBIG
The range passes the largest offset.
.It Bq Er EFBIG
The range grows the file past
.Dv RLIMIT_FSIZE ,
with
.Dv SIGXFSZ .
.It Bq Er ENOSPC
The file system has no room for the range, or a tmpfs's
.Ql size=
none.
.It Bq Er EPERM
A seal of a tmpfs file forbids the change, the file is immutable, or it
is append-only and
.Fa mode
has a flag but
.Dv FALLOC_FL_KEEP_SIZE .
.It Bq Er EOPNOTSUPP
.Fa fd
is a block device or a file of a file system without the operation, or
.Fa mode
is one its file system does not take.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ftruncate 2 ,
.Xr write 2
